 "one_screen": "Objective: read a mole ratio from a balanced equation's coefficients and use it to calculate a mass or mole quantity. Prerequisite: molar mass of \\ce{O2}? (O=16) → 32 g/mol. Worked example: $100$ g \\ce{Mg} reacts with excess \\ce{HCl} (\\ce{Mg + 2HCl -> MgCl2 + H2}) — moles \\ce{Mg} = $100/24=4.17$ mol; ratio \\ce{Mg}:\\ce{H2}=1:1, so moles \\ce{H2}=$4.17$; volume = $4.17\\times22.4=93.4$ dm³. Practice: (1) \\ce{2Mg + O2 -> 2MgO} — ratio \\ce{Mg}:\\ce{O2}? → 2:1. (2) \\ce{Fe2O3 + 3CO -> 2Fe + 3CO2}, moles \\ce{CO} for $5$ mol \\ce{Fe2O3}? → $15$ mol. (3) $4$ g \\ce{H2} + $32$ g \\ce{O2} — is $4:32$ the mole ratio? → No, convert to moles first: 2:1. Misconception: pupils read the ratio off a SUBSCRIPT (the 2 in \\ce{H2O}) instead of the COEFFICIENT in front of the formula — always ask which number is in front. Exit question: \\ce{2K + Cl2 -> 2KCl} — mole ratio \\ce{K}:\\ce{Cl2}? → 2:1."
}